{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Do not fear, etc., for I will make you a great nation, etc.</b> One needs to know what the fear was that Jacob had regarding his descent to Egypt, and if it was the fear of exile, then what was the intent of HaShem in saying ״אַל תִּירָא״ — by what does He remove the fear from him? And if His intent was that there would be no exile there, we find that this was not so, for there his sons were enslaved with affliction and hard labor. It is further difficult that He said <b>for I will make you a great nation</b> — what kind of reason is this for removing the fear? Further, one needs to know His saying <b>I will make you a great nation there</b> — and why would He not make him a great nation in another place? Further, one needs to know why He said <b>for a nation</b> (לְגוֹי) and did not say ״for a great nation I will make you״ (כִּי גוֹי גָּדוֹל אֲשִׂימְךָ) and so on." "<b>Now,</b> the intent of the verse is on account of what had preceded in the revelation to the fathers, that the decree of exile had been decreed upon them, as it is written (Genesis 15:13) ״כִּי גֵר יִהְיֶה זַרְעֲךָ״ and so on, and Abraham would presumably have told his children, and so it is explicit in the words of our Sages, whose memory is for blessing (Genesis Rabbah 82). Therefore, when Jacob saw that there was grain in Egypt, for there his sons would endure the exile, therefore when he went down he feared for himself that the exile would begin with him, and he was also afraid lest he be buried there in an impure land. Therefore HaShem appeared to him and said to him ״אָנֹכִי אֱלֹהֵי אָבִיךָ״ — the intent of this statement is that just as your father did not taste the taste of servitude and affliction, so too you — do not fear, even though you are going down to Egypt. And because Jacob might think to return and go up from Egypt when he finds a fitting opportunity, which is after the years of famine pass, and the reassurance that HaShem, blessed be He, gives him in saying ״אַל תִּירָא״ applies only to the brief time he would be there, therefore He concluded by saying <b>for I will make you a great nation there</b> — meaning, I wish to make you there for the purpose of a great nation." "To gain understanding of the matter, I will preface with the holy words of the Rashbi and the men of his mystery (Likkutei Torah, Vayeishev), who said that the reason for the exiles is to refine the portions of holiness scattered among the branches of the kelipah, and since Egypt was full of idols and there was the stronghold of impurity, there was the captivity of many and mighty portions of holiness, for proportionate to the greatness of the impurity would be the portions of holiness within it. And this had been so since the day the first man sinned, when the evil handmaiden gained dominion over the sparks of holiness, and the eyes of all Israel are upon this in all the exiles, and especially in the Egyptian exile. And they further said, their memory for blessing (Likkutei Torah, Vayeshev), that the people that went out from the land of Egypt and stood at Mount Sinai is the people that had been captive within the kelipah of Egypt, and to them Moses the prophet of HaShem said (Deuteronomy 4:8) ״וּמִי גּוֹי גָּדוֹל״ and so on. And to this very thing HaShem intends in speaking with Jacob: the reason He consents to Jacob's dwelling in Egypt is for the sake of the great nation that is lost there in the kelipah of Egypt and needs to be brought out from there, and without Jacob's descent there is no hope for this, for through the intensity of his holiness he draws up all the branches of holiness that are there, which no one other than him can do. And therefore you will find that they said, their memory for blessing (Genesis Rabbah 79), that our father Jacob did not die until he saw sixty myriads issuing from his loins, and those sixty myriads were afflicted there afterward and were refined in the iron crucible in the poverty of Egypt and emerged purified. And from the conclusion of the matter Jacob sensed that the Blessed One desires Jacob to dwell there, and with the assurance that no servitude or affliction would befall him personally, while he was still troubled about burial in that land, therefore He said ״אָנֹכִי אֵרֵד עִמְּךָ״ and so on, ״וְאָנֹכִי אַעַלְךָ״ and so on." "<b>Further,</b> He intends to tell him the reason that He desires the servitude and affliction to be fulfilled in Egypt — for through this the matter would be weighty upon his sons, in accord with <b>for a great nation</b> and so on that is there, and the matter is necessarily bound to be so."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:49:16.231988989Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}